Reactor Effect Client

This independent SDK is not an official Reactor SDK.

The canonical wire definitions and selected protocol behavior derive from:
- reactor-client-sdks, commit 24f1865b48f03354b15402731f1b92e90ceee92a
- reactor-runtime, commit 8e98536c6daedca298700dc45cbb5fd9e3676f16
Their upstream licenses and notices are retained under notices/.
fixtures/proto contains the selected canonical protocol sources. The generated
wire module also incorporates Google's protobuf Struct descriptor; see
notices/google-protobuf-LICENSE.

Session, correlation, observation and browser-media implementations incorporate
the user-supplied reactor-effect-client reference source. Reusable H3, coordinator,
reference and engine behavior is extracted from the user's the-show source.
The extracted package does not depend on the-show or ship its application policy.

The native transport is built from the Rust bridge in native/ against:
reactor-webrtc, commit bebf63e42624ee440e066b692494dd3229d29ae7 (Apache-2.0).
It incorporates upstream Google WebRTC binaries and associated third-party code.
The pinned reactor-webrtc Apache terms are retained in
notices/reactor-webrtc-LICENSE. Exact macOS arm64 and Linux x64 prebuilt SBOMs,
plus the pinned WebRTC BSD license and patent grant, are retained under
notices/reactor-webrtc/. Component-specific third-party redistribution texts
for every component named by those SBOMs are retained under
notices/reactor-webrtc/third-party/; its README names the exact pinned source
revision and retained license/copyright or patent file for each component.
The Rust crates linked into the native bridge and their selected license texts
are recorded under notices/native-rust/.
Native builds pin and verify the upstream binary artifact checksum.
No reactor-ffi or reactor-core session implementation is included.

Effect is MIT-licensed. The package declares Effect as a peer dependency; its
license is retained in notices/effect-LICENSE. Koffi is an optional dependency
used only by the explicit native implementation; its installed distribution
retains its upstream license. Development tooling and node_modules are excluded
from the npm package.

The reference archive's limited TypeScript WebRTC/codec implementation is not
part of this package's production graph. Retained owned-webrtc notices document
the supplied reference and vector provenance; they do not indicate that a second
WebRTC engine is shipped or supported.
